A||||11||FieroJones||3800SC   Questions||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJones||12-20-2001||08:16 PM||||Well, Basic Question, anyone dones this swap, or know of anything about it?  DOes the engine bolt up to the tranny, or do i need an adapter?  The Fiero needs more power and a friend recommended that this engine was his all time want, so I figured I'd find out as much as i can.  Anyone know what car they are in, what the power ratings are, rebuild costs for engine and Supercharger, etc etc etc..  Thanks in Advance!||||reg||1
Z||000001||GTDude||12-20-2001||08:27 PM||||The 3800 is not too big a problem.....adding the SC makes it more difficult....in fact the SC and belt have to sit in an opening in the wheel well.  I would consider it quite a challenge.  No adapers are needed tho but you must change the flywheel if it's a straight shift.  Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||12-20-2001||08:42 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTDude:<BR><B>The 3800 is not too big a problem.....adding the SC makes it more difficult....in fact the SC and belt have to sit in an opening in the wheel well.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Where did you get that information?<P>Here is a pic of Earl Sessions car with wheel well intact.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/engine_12.jpg"> <P><BR>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||12-20-2001||10:23 PM||||I've seen pics of a LOT of 3800SC swaps because I was thinking of doing one myself but decided to try a 3.4 DOHC. You <B>do not</B> have to run the belt in the fender. GTDude, you must be thinking of the Archie V8 swap.<P>The bellhousing will bolt up, the flywheel won't. If you're using an auto, it's better to swap the engine/tranny, wiring and PCM from the donor. Series II (96+) engines have more power (3800 ~205HP and 3800SC ~240HP, 280ft/lbs) You can get the SC to about 260-280HP by changing the SC pulley for a couple hundred bucks. You can probably get a good low mileage unit from the junk yard cheaper than getting an older one and rebuilding, unless you can rebuild it yourself.<P>Do a search in the tech section AND the Tech section archives here for "3800". You will be reading for HOURS. If you search the web for "3800 Fiero" you'll find a few sites. <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  (West Coast Fieros) has headers, a custom flywheel (if you use the Fiero manual tranny), mounts and other info.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||jg88gt||12-20-2001||11:06 PM||||I have got most of the the little details worked out on my son's now, run great completly diffrent car now. Pulls real strong, we still have a stock Red 88GT that has less then 50K on it the my son's Black 88GT 3800 SC would walk all over it. My son loved the 2.8 5 speed but now since we installed the 3800 SC with the 5 speed in his he tells me that our 2.8 stock GT is a old man's car. Here is a post from before with some pictures <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011450.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011450.html</A>  would do this swap again, it's what the car needed. This car only had about 65K on it before the swap and ran great.||||reg||6
Z||000005||fieroX||12-21-2001||03:26 AM||||L67's are awesome<P>------------------<BR>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif">  </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroX.com</A><BR>coming soon L67 powered 87 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000006||David DeVoe||12-21-2001||09:08 AM||||DRH, Not to nitpick but the Series II 3800 went into production for the 95 my.<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||12-21-2001||11:48 AM||||For the non-SC engine. SC engines in 95 were S1's.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fieroX||12-21-2001||11:19 PM||||the initial prototype series II SC was made in early 95.  It didnt go into production until 96, some cars were series I in 96 and some were series II.  Depending on whether they were early or late 96 models.<p>[This message has been edited by fieroX (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||12-22-2001||01:13 AM||||Every MY95 SC car I've seen was S1 and every MY96 was S2.<P>Let's talk model years, not calender years.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fieroX||12-22-2001||03:15 AM||||I just heard that the early model year 96 cars had series I engines, so I looked around a little and couldnt find anything so you are right Terry.  I did learn alot about the engine while looking around.  And the more I learn the more power I will make.||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||12-22-2001||01:16 PM||||Oh, I see what you mean, you are pushing the S1 out further. Hmm, I guess it's possible since GM likes to do rolling changes. It could be they did that on Federal cars, but not California cars. We seem to get changes a bit earlier for emissions reason (we had flash ECM's on DOHC's a year or so earlier than Fed cars.)<P>I have two friends with 96 SSEi's and both are S2's with 4T60E's. I have an S1 from a 95 SSEi and I know of two others 95's that were S1's.<P>Anyone have a non-California 96 with an S1 supercharged?||||reg||1
